            <div xml:id="d369">

                <pb facs="0752" n="666" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg666"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>No official record of this meeting has been found. Churchill (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hinge of Fate</hi>, pp. 678–679) describes his meeting
                    with Eaker, date unspecified, during which the latter stated the case for the
                    American daylight <pb facs="0753" n="667" xml:id="pg667"/> bombing procedure and
                    reviewed the preparations in England for the daylight bombing force. Churchill
                    records that he was persuaded to support Eaker and his views. According to
                    Eaker’s diary (Manuscripts Division, Library of Congress, Washington, D. C), he
                    arrived in Casablanca on January 16 and saw Churchill on January 20 for about
                    half an hour. Arnold (pp. 393 and 395–397) recalls that he asked Eaker to come
                    to Casablanca to help convince Churchill of the soundness of daylight bombing.
                    Arnold adds that on January 19 he told Churchill that he would like him to talk
                    with Generals Andrews, Spaatz, and Eaker, who were then in Casablanca, on the
                    daylight bombing matter, and Churchill did so on the “same day”. Eaker’s visit
                    is not mentioned in the Log or in the communiqué; Spaatz’s visit to Casablanca
                    is mentioned in the Log, <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg529"
                        >p. 529</ref>, and in the communiqué, <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>,
                        <ref target="#pg847">p. 847</ref>; Andrews’ visit to Casablanca is recorded
                    in the communiqué, p. 847.</p>
            </div>
